Fix multi-day session log loss in contest-log-collector

Target: contest-log-collector skill, tools/export-session.py
Tested against: claude-code-collector@1.2.0, manifest schema_version 1.0
Reported by: team contest2026_098_zhanshangxingguang, 2026-08-19

Problem
-------
copy_session() resolves the staging source file from the manifest entry's
file_path, which records a single date.  Staging files a session under the
calendar day its events occurred, so a session running past midnight has one
file per day:

    <staging>/<login>/2026-08-17/claude-code__<sid>.jsonl
    <staging>/<login>/2026-08-18/claude-code__<sid>.jsonl

Because the manifest holds only one of those dates, and because copy_session()
copies at most one file, two distinct failures occur:

  A. The recorded date matches no staging file.  The tool prints
     "source jsonl not found" and returns rc=1.  Visible, but the wording
     suggests the session was never captured, when the file is in fact sitting
     in staging under a different date.

  B. The recorded date matches one of several files.  Only that day is
     exported; the remaining days are dropped while the tool reports success
     and returns rc=0.  This case is silent.

Measured on one team's staging area on 2026-08-19: 8 of 14 sessions affected.
Seven hit case A.  One hit case B -- session 2911cf58 has 478 events on
2026-08-10 and 31 on 2026-08-11; the manifest pointed at 08-11, so the tool
reported success while dropping 478 of the session's 509 events.

The loss is not detectable by validate-log.py.  Running the unpatched tool with
`--all --confirm` into a clean destination exports 7 of 19 per-day files and
316 of 12852 events -- 2.5% of the captured content -- and validate-log.py then
reports "ALL OK" with zero errors and zero warnings, because the manifest it
wrote and the files it copied are mutually consistent.  A team following the
documented workflow exactly would submit 2.5% of its logs and see a green
check.

After the patch, the same command exports all 19 files, byte-identical to
staging, and validate-log.py reports 0 errors.

Fix
---
Resolve sources by globbing on the session id instead of trusting the recorded
date, and copy every matching file.  This makes export independent of how the
manifest happens to date a session.

Deliberately NOT changed
------------------------
event_count stays scoped to the single file that file_path points at, because
validate-log.py enforces `event_count == line count of the file at file_path`.
Setting it to the session total across days makes the validator fail with
"event_count mismatch" (verified).  Note that staging's own manifest uses
session totals for the same field, so the two sides of the tooling disagree on
its meaning -- worth reconciling separately.

The earlier-day files of a multi-day session therefore remain unreferenced by
the manifest, which validate-log.py reports as "orphan file" warnings (5 of
them in the case above).  The data is present and complete; only the
cross-reference is missing.  Representing a multi-day session properly needs a
schema change, e.g. a list of files per session entry, which this patch does
not attempt.

Apply and verify
----------------
Run from the skill root (the directory containing tools/):

    patch -p1 < contest-log-collector-multiday.patch

Verify against staging without touching a real repo:

    mkdir -p /tmp/verify/logs
    python3 tools/export-session.py --all --confirm --dest /tmp/verify
    diff -r <staging>/<login> /tmp/verify/logs/<login> \
         --exclude=manifest.json --exclude=errors
    python3 tools/validate-log.py /tmp/verify/logs

Expected: diff reports no differences, validator reports 0 errors.  The
validator will report one "orphan file" warning per extra day of every
multi-day session; see "Deliberately NOT changed" above.

